::ALG1_U03_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x - 4 < 9. {
=A. x < 13#Correct. Add 4 to both sides.
~B. x < 5#This subtracts 4 instead of adding 4.
~C. x > 13#The direction does not change when adding 4.
~D. x > 5#This uses the wrong operation and direction.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 5x <= 20. {
~A. x <= 25#This adds 5 instead of dividing by 5.
~B. x >= 4#The direction does not reverse when dividing by positive 5.
=C. x <= 4#Correct. Divide both sides by 5.
~D. x >= 15#This subtracts 5 instead of dividing.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 3x + 2 > 14. {
~A. x > 12#This stops after subtracting 2 and does not divide by 3.
=B. x > 4#Correct. Subtract 2 to get 3x > 12, then divide by 3.
~C. x < 4#The direction does not reverse when dividing by positive 3.
~D. x > 16#This adds 2 instead of subtracting 2.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -2x > 10. {
~A. x > -5#This forgets to reverse the inequality after dividing by -2.
~B. x < 5#The boundary sign is wrong.
~C. x > 5#This has both the wrong sign and wrong direction.
=D. x < -5#Correct. Divide by -2 and reverse the symbol.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph represents x >= 6? {
=A. Closed circle at 6, shade right#Correct. The boundary is included and values greater than 6 are solutions.
~B. Open circle at 6, shade right#This would represent x > 6, not x >= 6.
~C. Closed circle at 6, shade left#This would represent x <= 6.
~D. Open circle at 6, shade left#This would represent x < 6.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -4x <= 8. {
~A. x <= -2#This finds the boundary but forgets to reverse the symbol.
=B. x >= -2#Correct. Divide by -4 and reverse the inequality.
~C. x <= 2#The boundary sign is wrong.
~D. x >= 2#This has the wrong boundary sign.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2 <= x + 5 < 11. {
~A. 7 <= x < 16#This adds 5 instead of subtracting 5 from all parts.
~B. -3 < x <= 6#This reverses which endpoint is included.
~C. x <= -3 or x > 6#This changes a compound interval into an or statement.
=D. -3 <= x < 6#Correct. Subtract 5 from all three parts.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student has at most $40. A ticket costs $8. Which inequality describes the number of tickets t the student can buy? {
~A. t + 8 <= 40#This adds ticket count and dollars instead of multiplying.
~B. 8t >= 40#At most means the total should be less than or equal to 40.
~C. t <= 8 + 40#This does not model repeated $8 tickets.
=D. 8t <= 40#Correct. The total cost is 8 times the number of tickets and cannot exceed 40.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y + 7 >= 3. {
=A. y >= -4#Correct. Subtract 7 from both sides.
~B. y >= 10#This adds 7 instead of subtracting 7.
~C. y <= -4#The direction does not change when subtracting 7.
~D. y <= 10#This uses the wrong operation and direction.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 6 - x < 2. {
~A. x < -4#This misses the direction reversal after dividing by -1.
~B. x > -8#This uses the wrong boundary.
~C. x < 4#This is the boundary but wrong direction.
=D. x > 4#Correct. Subtract 6 to get -x < -4, then divide by -1 and reverse.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph represents x < -1? {
~A. Closed circle at -1, shade left#Closed circle would include -1.
=B. Open circle at -1, shade left#Correct. Less than uses an open circle and shades left.
~C. Open circle at -1, shade right#This represents x > -1.
~D. Closed circle at -1, shade right#This represents x >= -1.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 4x - 9 <= 7. {
~A. x <= -4#This subtracts 9 instead of adding 9.
~B. x >= 4#The direction does not reverse when dividing by positive 4.
=C. x <= 4#Correct. Add 9 to get 4x <= 16, then divide by 4.
~D. x >= -4#This uses the wrong operation and direction.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -5n < 15. {
=A. n > -3#Correct. Divide by -5 and reverse the inequality.
~B. n < -3#This forgets to reverse the symbol.
~C. n > 3#The boundary sign is wrong.
~D. n < 3#This has the wrong boundary sign and direction.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -2 < x - 1 <= 4. {
~A. -3 < x <= 3#This subtracts 1 instead of adding 1.
~B. -1 <= x < 5#This reverses which endpoint is included.
=C. -1 < x <= 5#Correct. Add 1 to all three parts.
~D. x < -1 or x >= 5#This changes the compound inequality into an or statement.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A plan costs $12 plus $3 per day. Which inequality represents spending less than $30 for d days? {
~A. 12d + 3 < 30#This reverses the fixed fee and daily rate.
~B. 3d + 12 > 30#The phrase less than means the symbol should be <.
~C. d + 15 < 30#This adds the numbers instead of modeling the daily rate.
=D. 3d + 12 < 30#Correct. Total cost is 3d + 12 and it must be less than 30.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 7x >= -21. {
~A. x <= -3#The direction does not reverse when dividing by positive 7.
=B. x >= -3#Correct. Divide both sides by 7.
~C. x >= -14#This adds 7 instead of dividing.
~D. x <= 3#This uses the wrong sign and direction.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes x <= 2? {
=A. Closed circle at 2, shade left#Correct. The value 2 is included and smaller values are solutions.
~B. Open circle at 2, shade left#Open circle would not include 2.
~C. Closed circle at 2, shade right#This represents x >= 2.
~D. Open circle at 2, shade right#This represents x > 2.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 10 < 2x. {
~A. x < 5#Dividing by positive 2 keeps the direction; 10 < 2x means 5 < x.
=B. x > 5#Correct. Divide both sides by 2 to get 5 < x, or x > 5.
~C. x > 8#This subtracts 2 instead of dividing by 2.
~D. x < 12#This adds 2 instead of dividing by 2.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -3 <= x + 2 < 6. {
~A. -1 <= x < 8#This adds 2 instead of subtracting 2.
~B. -5 < x <= 4#This reverses which endpoint is included.
=C. -5 <= x < 4#Correct. Subtract 2 from all three parts.
~D. x <= -5 or x > 4#This changes the interval into an or statement.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student needs at least 24 points. Each question is worth 3 points. Which inequality models q questions answered correctly? {
~A. q + 3 >= 24#This adds the point value instead of multiplying.
~B. 3q <= 24#At least means greater than or equal to, not less than or equal to.
=C. 3q >= 24#Correct. The total points are 3q and must be at least 24.
~D. q >= 3 + 24#This does not model points per question.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2x + 1 <= -7. {
=A. x <= -4#Correct. Subtract 1 to get 2x <= -8, then divide by 2.
~B. x >= -4#The direction does not reverse when dividing by positive 2.
~C. x <= -3#This subtracts incorrectly before dividing.
~D. x >= 4#This uses the wrong sign and direction.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve -x >= 11. {
~A. x >= -11#This forgets to reverse the symbol after dividing by -1.
~B. x <= 11#The boundary sign is wrong.
=C. x <= -11#Correct. Divide by -1 and reverse the inequality.
~D. x >= 11#This has both the wrong sign and wrong direction.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which inequality matches an open circle at 3 shaded to the right? {
~A. x >= 3#Closed circle would include 3.
=B. x > 3#Correct. Open circle excludes 3 and shading right means greater than.
~C. x < 3#This would shade left.
~D. x <= 3#This would use a closed circle and shade left.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 1 <= 3x + 1 < 10. {
~A. 0 <= x < 9#This subtracts 1 but does not divide by 3.
~B. 2 <= x < 11#This adds 1 instead of subtracting it.
~C. 0 < x <= 3#This reverses which endpoint is included.
=D. 0 <= x < 3#Correct. Subtract 1 from all parts, then divide by 3.




}

::ALG1_U03_L06_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.AR.2<br><br>Question:<br>A budget is at most $50. A service costs $14 plus $6 per item. Which inequality and solution match? {
=A. 6x + 14 <= 50; x <= 6#Correct. Subtract 14 to get 6x <= 36, then divide by 6.
~B. 14x + 6 <= 50; x <= 3#This reverses the fixed cost and item rate.
~C. 6x + 14 >= 50; x >= 6#At most means less than or equal to.
~D. x + 20 <= 50; x <= 30#This combines the cost numbers instead of using the rate.




}
